{"records":[{"id":"54v7hn8hvz","title":"Legionella pneumophila exploits the endo-lysosomal network for phagosome biogenesis","description":"L. pneumophila strains harboring wild-type rpsL such as Lp02rpsLWT cannot replicate in mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages (BMDMs) due to induction of extensive lysosome damage and apoptosis. The mechanism of this unique infection-induced cell death remains unknown. Using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screening, we identified Hmg20a and Nol9 as host factors important for restricting strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Depletion of Hmg20a protects macrophages from infection-induced lysosomal damage and apoptosis, allowing productive bacterial replication. The restriction imposed by Hmg20a was mediated by repressing the expression of several endo-lysosomal proteins, including the small GTPase Rab7. We found that SUMOylated Rab7 is recruited to the bacterial phagosome via SulF, a Dot/Icm effector that harbors a SUMO-interacting motif (SIM). Moreover, overexpression of Rab7 rescues intracellular growth of strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Our results establish that L. pneumophila exploits the lysosomal network for the biogenesis of its phagosome in BMDMs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-07T16:49:43.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54v7hn8hvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54v7hn8hvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sctxy3sm9w","title":"Generation and characterization of human iPSC-derived NPC1I1061T/I10161T i3Neurons as a model for NPC1 disease","description":"This manuscript describes the generation of an inducible pluripotent stem cell (iPSC) line expressing a mutant form of NPC1. This variant, NPC1(I1061T/I1061T), represents a common pathogenic variant of NPC1 in NPC1 affected individuals. Upon differentiation of the iPSCs into i3Neurons, we characterized the cellular phenotype with respect to gycosylation of NPC1, accumulation of free cholesterol, and response to known therapeutics. The results demonstrate that this cell line exhibits the expected characteristics of the NPC1(I1061/I1061T) mutant protein resulting in an NPC1 disease phenotype in these cells. This new cell line can be used as a neuronal model of Niemann-Pick disease, type C1.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-04T20:45:50.416Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sctxy3sm9w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sctxy3sm9w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Niamh","last_name":"Cawley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Forbes","last_name":"Porter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shikha","last_name":"Salhotra","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"NICHD Intramural Research Program of the National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"ZIA HD008988"}],"categories":["Niemann-Pick Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fw3g6w68xk","title":"miR-25","description":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) is the main pathogen causing tuberculosis (TB). Although autophagy is an important regulatory pathway to eliminate intracellular Mtb, the effect of NPC1 during Mtb infection remains largely unclear. Here, we found that microRNA-25 (miR-25) expression was significantly upregulated during in the lung tissues of mice infected with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) and macrophages infected with Mtb or BCG, especially in the early stages of infection. We validated that miR-25 targets the NPC1 protein located on the lysosomal membrane, resulting in damage to lysosomal function, thereby inhibiting autophagolysosome formation and promoting the survival of Mtb and BCG. Consistently, mice lacking miR-25 exhibited more resistant to BCG infection. In addition, we found that Rv1759c induces the expression of miR-25 through NFKB inhibitor zeta (NFKBIZ). Together, we reveal a new mechanism by which Mtb regulates NPC1 to influence lysosomal function and increase the survival of Mtb through the autophagy pathway.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-22T14:30:09.255Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fw3g6w68xk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fw3g6w68xk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenqi","last_name":"dong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["China"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t5xjphwzrg","title":"source data","description":"We found that microRNA-25 (miR-25) expression was significantly upregulated in the lung tissues of mice infected with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) and macrophages infected with Mtb or BCG, especially in the early stages of infection. MiR-25 can significantly increase the survival of Mtb and BCG in macrophages. We validated that miR-25 targets the NPC1 protein located on the lysosomal membrane, resulting in damage to lysosomal function, thereby inhibiting autophagolysosome formation and promoting the survival of Mtb and BCG. We demonstrated that mice lacking miR-25 are more resistant to BCG infection. In addition, we found that Rv1759c induces the expression of miR-25 through NFKBIZ. Together, we reveal a new mechanism by which Mtb regulates NPC1 to influence lysosomal function and increase the survival of Mtb through the autophagy pathway, providing an early biomarker for TB infection and a new treatment strategy against TB.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-22T14:30:03.873Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t5xjphwzrg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t5xjphwzrg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenqi","last_name":"dong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mhjpvbr7s8","title":"Lysosomal acid lipase regulates cholesterol metabolism during phagosomal maturation","description":"Phagocytosis, a central process in innate immunity, depends on dynamic lipid remodelling, yet how\ncholesterol accumulates on late phagosomes remains unresolved. Here, we identify lysosomal\nacid lipase (LAL) as a key cholesterol ester (CE) hydrolase driving cholesterol mobilization during\nphagosomal maturation. Using integrated lipidomics, chemoproteomics, and biochemical assays,\nwe show that LAL exhibits acidic CE hydrolase activity enriched on late phagosomes, generating\nfree cholesterol essential for lipid raft formation. Pharmacologically inhibiting and genetically\ndepleting LAL disrupts cholesterol-rich lipid raft assembly, impairs phagosomal trafficking, and\nalters pathogen fate – enhancing Staphylococcus aureus persistence, while restricting \nMycobacterium tuberculosis survival. These findings reveal that LAL couples cholesterol\nmetabolism to phagocytosis, defining a mechanistic link between lipid catabolism and antimicrobial\ndefence. By positioning CE hydrolysis as a critical determinant of phagosomal dynamics, our work\nuncovers a metabolic checkpoint in innate immunity and identifies LAL as a potential therapeutic\nnode in infection and inflammation.\n\nMajor findings of the study: Lalistat2/Lysosomal acid lipase knockdown has a pronounced effect on the levels of cholesterol and cholesteryl esters on the phagosomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-16T01:57:39.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mhjpvbr7s8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mhjpvbr7s8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Siddhesh","last_name":"Kamat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ojal","last_name":"Saharan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Mass Spectrometry","Lipidomics","Phagocytosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"89zgg5mw43","title":"ISCIENCE","description":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb), is the main pathogen causing tuberculosis (TB). Autophagy is an important regulatory pathway to eliminate intracellular Mtb. However, how Mtb escapes autophagy clearance remains largely unclear. Here, we found that microRNA-25 (miR-25) expression was significantly upregulated in the lung tissues of mice infected with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) and macrophages infected with Mtb or BCG, especially in the early stages of infection. MiR-25 can significantly increase the survival of Mtb and BCG in macrophages. We validated that miR-25 targets the NPC1 protein located on the lysosomal membrane, resulting in damage to lysosomal function, thereby inhibiting autophagolysosome formation and promoting the survival of Mtb and BCG. Consistently, mice lacking miR-25 exhibited more resistant to BCG infection. In addition, we found that Rv1759c induces the expression of miR-25 through NFKB inhibitor zeta (NFKBIZ). Together, we reveal a new mechanism by which Mtb regulates NPC1 to influence lysosomal function and increase the survival of Mtb through the autophagy pathway, providing an early biomarker for TB infection and a new treatment strategy against TB.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-12-01T12:23:30.693Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/89zgg5mw43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/89zgg5mw43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenqi","last_name":"dong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zmyr2jj8v6","title":"Exportin 1 pathway inhibition up-regulates nuclear and mitochondrial localization of dengue virus non-structural protein 3 ","description":"Supplementary data showing controls for exportin 1 (XPO1)-dependent nuclear export inhibition with Leptomycin B and Selinexor. Representative figures show nuclear retention of the cellular protein Cyclin B1, which is exported from the nucleus via XPO1, indicating that the drugs Leptomycin B and Selinexor specifically inhibit the XPO1 nuclear export pathway.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-22T10:37:18.988Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zmyr2jj8v6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zmyr2jj8v6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Selvin","last_name":"Palacios","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosa M","last_name":"del Angel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrial Transport","Nuclear Export","Dengue Virus","Exportin 1 Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nmz94tcppx","title":"ApoE4-dependent lysosomal cholesterol accumulation impairs mitochondrial homeostasis and oxidative phosphorylation in human astrocytes (2/2)","description":"Proteomics data from hiPSC-derived astrocytes (APOE3, APOE4, APOE4 + MβCD treatment)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-03T11:12:54.902Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nmz94tcppx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nmz94tcppx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jinsoo","last_name":"Seo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"22-BR-02-03"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"2022R1A2B5B03001929"},{"id":"https://ror.org/055zd7d59","name":"Korea Brain Research Institute","grant_id":"21-BR-03-02"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03frjya69","name":"Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology","grant_id":"22-CoE-BT-01"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"KSN2022230"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"2018M3C7A1056275"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00fdzyk40","name":"Korea Health Industry Development Institute","grant_id":"HU21C0111"}],"categories":["Neuroscience"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f6tx9z6ybs","title":"ApoE4-dependent lysosomal cholesterol accumulation impairs mitochondrial homeostasis and oxidative phosphorylation in human astrocytes (1/2)","description":"Proteomics data from hiPSC-derived astrocytes (APOE3, APOE4, APOE4 + MβCD treatment)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-03T11:15:50.613Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f6tx9z6ybs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f6tx9z6ybs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jinsoo","last_name":"Seo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00fdzyk40","name":"Korea Health Industry Development Institute","grant_id":"HU21C0111"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"2018M3C7A1056275"},{"id":"https://ror.org/055zd7d59","name":"Korea Brain Research Institute","grant_id":"21-BR-03-02"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"2022R1A2B5B03001929"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"KSN2022230"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03frjya69","name":"Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology","grant_id":"22-CoE-BT-01"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01wpjm123","name":"Ministry of Science and ICT","grant_id":"22-BR-02-03"}],"categories":["Neuroscience"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hp5wwtjsnz","title":"Hnf1aos1 as a Metabolic Coordinator of Hepatic Lipid Homeostasis and Feedback Control-Raw qPCR Data ","description":"Abstract: Long noncoding RNAs (lncRNAs) have emerged as critical regulators of hepatic metabolism and disease progression. The hepatocyte nuclear factor 1 alpha antisense 1 (HNF1A-AS1) lncRNA modulates liver‑specific transcription factors; however, its physiological role in diet‑dependent lipid homeostasis remains poorly defined. In this study, we investigated the mouse ortholog, Hnf1aos1, using AAV-mediated knockdown in C57BL/6J mice fed either a chow diet (10% kcal from fat) or a high‑fat diet (HFD; 60% kcal from fat) for 12 weeks. Metabolic phenotyping included hepatic lipid quantification, histological analysis, serum biochemistry, and quantitative gene expression profiling. Loss of Hnf1aos1 produced distinct, diet‑dependent alterations in hepatic lipid handling. Under chow conditions, knockdown mice exhibited selective hepatic cholesterol accumulation (6.10 ± 2.9 mg/g tissue vs. 3.51 ± 1.1 mg/g in controls), accompanied by dysregulation of cholesterol clearance pathways. In contrast, under HFD conditions, knockdown precipitated severe macrovesicular degeneration, with hepatic triglyceride levels approximately doubled relative to HFD‑fed controls (51.72 ± 19.8 mg/g vs. 26.34 ± 11.9 mg/g) and a markedly elevated triglyceride‑to‑cholesterol ratio (TG:TC ≈ 6.1:1). Chow/Kd mice gained sig-nificantly less weight than chow‑fed controls, whereas HFD/Kd mice exhibited weight gain comparable to HFD controls despite severe hepatic steatosis. This paradoxical phenotype suggests impaired metabolic feedback, in which compensatory upregulation of Hnf1a transcription is associated with increased ex-pression of lipid‑associated genes such as Cd36, despite profound lipid overload. Collectively, these findings identify Hnf1aos1 as a regulator of hepatic lipid homeostasis whose loss produces a phenotype consistent with inappropriate lipid accumulation during nutrient excess, without defining the underlying molecular mechanism. Our results support a role for Hnf1aos1 in shaping hepatic metabolic plasticity and provide insight into lncRNA‑associated MASLD phenotypes. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-16T21:33:53.602Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hp5wwtjsnz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hp5wwtjsnz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Beshoy","last_name":"Armanios","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Real-Time Polymerase Chain Reaction"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":81121,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":130,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":81121,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":80882,"restricted":48,"embargoed":191},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1304},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":321},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":296},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":280},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":274},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":251},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":211},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":200},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":160}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":78933},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1686},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":143},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":56},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":47},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":42},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":24},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":21}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":357},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":294},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":281},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":217},{"id":"a96fae95-4d57-5e87-95d2-fc35ad11b0ca","name":"Universidad Cooperativa de Colombia","count":213}]}}